# Line-ending policy: LF everywhere, in the repo AND in the working tree.
#
# Why this file exists: development happens on Windows, CI runs on Linux, and
# Docker images are Linux. Without a policy, git's core.autocrlf rewrites files
# on checkout while the `mixed-line-ending` pre-commit hook rewrites them back
# to LF, so the two fight each other and every file shows as modified. Git even
# warns about it directly:
#
#   warning: in the working copy of 'CLAUDE.md', LF will be replaced by CRLF
#            the next time Git touches it
#
# Line endings are git's job, not a linter's. `text=auto eol=lf` tells git to
# detect text files and use LF on both sides, which ends the churn. The
# pre-commit hook then becomes a harmless second opinion rather than a
# combatant.
#
# Practical payoff: a one-line change shows up as a one-line diff instead of a
# whole-file rewrite, and shell scripts copied into a Linux container do not
# fail with the classic `\r: command not found`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary: never touch these, and never try to diff them as text.
*.parquet binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pkl binary
*.joblib binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary

# Generated lockfile: mark as a diff-suppressed generated file so reviews are
# not swamped by thousands of hash lines. Still fully version controlled.
uv.lock linguist-generated=true -diff
